WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.960
Reinforcement learning is an awesome algorithm
that is able to play computer games, navigate

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:15.490
helicopters, hit a baseball, or even defeat
Go champions when combined together with a

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:17.949
neural network and Monte Carlo tree search.

00:00:17.949 --> 00:00:23.220
It is a quite general algorithm that is able
to take on a variety of difficult problems

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.609
that involve observing an environment and
coming up with a series of actions to maximize

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:29.619
a score.

00:00:29.619 --> 00:00:34.380
In a previous episode, we had a look at DeepMind's
algorithm where a set of movement actions

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:39.140
had to be chosen to navigate in a difficult
3D environment efficiently.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.980
The score to be maximized was the distance
measured from the starting point, the further

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.530
our character went, the higher score it was
given, and it has successfully learned the

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:50.390
concept of locomotion.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.390
Really cool!

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:56.190
A prerequisite for a reinforcement learner
to work properly is that it has to be given

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.500
informative reward signals.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.120
For instance, if we go to a written exam,
as an output, we would like to get a detailed

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.990
breakdown of the number of points we got for
each problem.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:11.700
This way, we know where we did well and which
kinds of problems need some more work.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.469
However, imagine having a really careless
teacher who never tells us the points, but

00:01:16.469 --> 00:01:20.119
would only tell us whether we have failed
of passed.

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:25.869
No explanation, no points for individual tasks,
no telling whether we failed by a lot or just

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:27.170
by a tiny bit.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.170
Nothing.

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.880
First attempt, we failed.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.490
Next time, we failed again.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.409
And again and again and again.

00:01:33.409 --> 00:01:38.509
Now this would be a dreadful learning experience
because we would have absolutely no idea what

00:01:38.509 --> 00:01:39.509
to improve.

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:42.000
Clearly, this teacher would have to be fired.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.299
However, when formulating a reinforcement
learning problem, instead of using more informative

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:52.159
scores, it is much easier to just tell whether
the algorithm was successful or not.

00:01:52.159 --> 00:01:55.780
It is very convenient for us to be this careless
teacher.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.639
Otherwise, what score would make sense for
a helicopter control problem when we almost

00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:02.139
crash into a tree?

00:02:02.139 --> 00:02:07.179
This part is called reward engineering and
the main issue is that we have to adapt the

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:12.689
problem to the algorithm, where the best would
be if the algorithm would adapt to the problem.

00:02:12.689 --> 00:02:17.900
This has been a long-standing problem in reinforcement
learning research, and a potential solution

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:23.290
would open up the possibility of solving even
harder and more interesting problems with

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:24.870
learning algorithms.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:30.549
And this is exactly the what researchers at
OpenAI try to solve by introducing Hindsight

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:35.810
Experience Replay, HER, or her in short.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:36.810
Very apt.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.450
This algorithm takes on problems where the
scores are binary, which means that it either

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:44.000
passed or failed the prescribed task.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.659
A classic careless teacher scenario.

00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:52.390
And these rewards are not only binary, but
very sparse as well, which further exacerbates

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.040
the difficulty of the problem.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.920
In the video, you can see a comparison with
a previous algorithm with and without the

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.360
HER extension.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:05.290
The higher the number of epochs you see above,
the longer the algorithm was able to train.

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.549
The incredible thing here is that it is able
to achieve a goal even if it had never been

00:03:10.549 --> 00:03:12.650
able to reach it during training.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:18.620
The key idea is that we can learn just as
much from undesirable outcomes as from desirable

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.620
ones.

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:20.989
Let me quote the authors.

00:03:20.989 --> 00:03:25.010
Imagine that you are learning how to play
hockey and are trying to shoot a puck into

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:26.010
a net.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.840
You hit the puck but it misses the net on
the right side.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.689
The conclusion drawn by a standard reinforcement
learning algorithm in such a situation would

00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:39.170
be that the performed sequence of actions
does not lead to a successful shot, and little

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:40.719
(if anything) would be learned.

00:03:40.719 --> 00:03:46.290
It is however possible to draw another conclusion,
namely that this sequence of actions would

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.109
be successful if the net had been placed further
to the right.

00:03:50.109 --> 00:03:55.359
They have achieved this by storing and replaying
previous experiences with different potential

00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:56.359
goals.

00:03:56.359 --> 00:03:59.690
As always, the details are available in the
paper, make sure to have a look.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:05.060
Now, it is always good to test things out
whether the whole system works well in software,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.610
however, its usefulness has been demonstrated
by deploying it on a real robot arm.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.310
You can see the goal written on the screen
alongside with the results.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:19.660
A really cool piece of work that can potentially
open up new ways of thinking about reinforcement

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:20.660
learning.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.979
After all, it's great to have learning algorithms
that are so good, they can solve problems

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:29.100
that we formulate in such a lazy way that
we'd have to be fired.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:34.069
